{"name":"Forest Shade","hex":"#91ac80","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"forest","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dense uncultivated tract of trees and undergrowth, larger than woods.","Any dense collection or amount."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover an area with trees."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/forest"},{"term":"shade","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Darkness where light, particularly sunlight, is blocked.","Something that blocks light, particularly in a window."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To shield (someone or something) from light.","To shield oneself from light."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shade"}],"entries":[{"title":"Forest","blocks":[{"text":"A forest is an ecosystem characterized by a dense community of trees. Hundreds of definitions of forest are used throughout the world, incorporating factors such as tree density, tree height, land use, legal standing, and ecological function. The United Nations' Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) defines a forest as, \"Land spanning more than 0.5 hectares with trees higher than 5 meters and a canopy cover of more than 10 percent, or trees able to reach these thresholds in situ. It does not include land that is predominantly under agricultural or urban use.\" Using this definition, Global Forest Resources Assessment 2025 found that forests covered 4.14 billion hectares, or approximately 31 percent of the world's land area in 2025."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Aerial_view_of_the_Amazon_Rainforest.jpg/330px-Aerial_view_of_the_Amazon_Rainforest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forest","wikidata":"Q4421"},{"title":"Tint, shade and tone","blocks":[{"text":"In color theory, a tint is a mixture of a color with white, which increases lightness, while a shade is a mixture with black, which increases darkness. A tone is produced either by mixing a color with gray, or by both tinting and shading. Mixing a color with any neutral color reduces the chroma, or colorfulness, while the perceived hue can be affected slightly."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Tint-tone-shade.svg/330px-Tint-tone-shade.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tint%2C_shade_and_tone","wikidata":"Q2749750"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg/500px-Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg/1280px-Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg/500px-Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The heavily traversed Adirondack Mountains of Upstate New York form the southernmost part of the Eastern forest-boreal transition ecoregion, constituting part of the world's taiga biome.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAdirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Niepolomice_oli_2013251.jpg/500px-Niepolomice_oli_2013251.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Niepolomice_oli_2013251.jpg/500px-Niepolomice_oli_2013251.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Since the 13th century, the Niepołomice Forest in Poland has had special use and protection. In this view from spa